H2("Modality structure is clear within the untrained and Kvasir-trained groups");
P("Within the untrained and Kvasir-trained groups, datasets of the same modality agree more than datasets of different modalities: the modality difference is {{a.dice.U.mdiff}} (p = {{p.dice.U.t}}) and {{a.dice.K.mdiff}} (p = {{p.dice.K}}) (Table 3). Both groups are above the floor on most non-wound datasets. The from-scratch group shows a similar difference ({{a.dice.F.mdiff}}, p = {{p.dice.F}}), but its group mean Dice is below {{d.floor}} on {{md.F.nfloor}} of the seven non-wound datasets, so, as for the modern group, its rankings there order near-zero scores and we do not count it as evidence. For the untrained and Kvasir-trained groups the difference holds without the three within-source pairs ({{d52.UK.range}}, p at most {{p52.UK.max}}), under NSD ({{mdiff.nsd.UK.range}}, p at most {{p.nsd.UK.max}}) and with empty masks excluded ({{ne.UK.range}}, p at most {{ne.UK.pmax}}; Online Resource 1, Tables S2b, S2e and S2f). Colonoscopy datasets from three sources agree at {{a.dice.U.colon}} and {{a.dice.K.colon}} within these two groups, whereas the wound datasets agree at {{a.dice.U.wound}} and {{a.dice.K.wound}}.", { after: 100 });
P("For the modern models the same statistic cannot be interpreted. Their group mean Dice is at most {{md.M19.other.max}} on each of the seven non-wound datasets, so their rankings there order near-zero scores. The modality difference among the {{n.M19}} modern models is {{a.dice.M19.mdiff}} (p = {{p.dice.M19}}), but it varied with the training seed: for the ten original architectures with nnU-Net it is {{sdg.M.mdiff}} with the seed-{{d.seed0}} models, {{sdg.Ms1.mdiff}} with the seed-{{d.seed1}} models and {{sdg.Ms2.mdiff}} with the seed-{{d.seed2}} models (nine architectures), and {{a.dice.N8s.mdiff}} for the eight new architectures (exploratory; exact p {{p.dice.M}}, {{p.Ms1}}, {{p.Ms2}} and {{p.dice.N8s}}, uncorrected). We therefore do not count the modern group as evidence of modality structure.");

H2("Synthetic benchmark validity follows the method pool");
P("Generator B at texture amplitude {{d.amp.hi}} agrees with the four wound datasets at {{b.UM19.mean.bench}} ({{b.UM19.mean.bench.m}}) over the pooled untrained and modern methods, a value that would usually be read as good validation (Fig. 2, Table 4). Among the modern models alone it agrees at {{b.M19.mean.bench}} ({{b.M19.mean.bench.m}}), and the wound datasets agree with each other at {{b.M19.mean.other}}. The difference is {{b.M19.mean.diff}} ({{b.M19.mean.diff.m}}; hierarchical {{bh.M19.mean.diff}}), an interval too wide to say whether the benchmark is a better or a worse proxy than another wound dataset. The benchmark itself ranks the modern models reproducibly (mean agreement between its three synthetic seeds {{rv.syn.seedagree}}), so its low agreement with the wound datasets is not due to its {{d.syn.n}}-image size. Over the pooled set the benchmark agrees with each wound dataset less than the other wound datasets do (difference {{b.UM19.mean.diff}} on average, {{b.UM19.mean.diff.m}}; methods resampled only, because untrained methods have no per-image synthetic scores). Among untrained methods it agrees at {{b.U.mean.bench}} ({{b.U.mean.bench.m}}); for the from-scratch and Kvasir-trained groups the intervals include zero.", { after: 100 });
P("Validity per reference varies, although single per-reference values are imprecise. Among Kvasir-trained methods the benchmark agrees with AZH at {{b.K.azh.bench}} and with FUSeg at {{b.K.fus.bench}} (difference {{rv.bd.K}}, {{rv.bd.K.m}}). Among untrained methods it agrees with CO2Wounds-V2 at {{b.U.co2.bench}} and with AZH at {{b.U.azh.bench}} (difference {{rv.bd.U}}, {{rv.bd.U.m}}). Among modern models its agreement with single wound datasets ranges from {{b.M19.range}} (Table 4). Reported against a single dataset, the same benchmark and pool could be described as valid or invalid.", { after: 100 });
P("Background texture raised validity only in pooled sets, and not in all of them. Moving generator B from amplitude {{d.amp.lo}} to {{d.amp.hi}} raises mean benchmark agreement by {{tx.UM19}} ({{tx.UM19.m}}) in U+M19, with every per-reference interval above zero, and by {{tx.UF}} ({{tx.UF.m}}) in U+F, but not in U+K ({{tx.UK}}, {{tx.UK.m}}). Within the groups the change is {{tx.U}}, {{tx.F}}, {{tx.K}} and {{tx.M19}} (U, F, K and M19), and only {{tx.within.n}} of the {{tx.within.tot}} per-reference intervals excludes zero; with no effect about {{tx.expect}} would be expected, although the {{tx.within.tot}} intervals are not independent.", { after: 100 });
P("Benchmark agreement among modern models also varied with the training seed. With single-seed versions of the original architectures ({{w.P10.n}} models; {{w.P10s2.n}} at seed {{d.seed2}}) it ranges from {{bs.P10.range}} over the three seeds, for the new architectures ({{w.N8.n}} models; {{w.N8s02.n}} at seeds {{d.seeds02}}) from {{bs.N8.range}}, and for the full single-seed modern group from {{bs.M19.range}}; the agreement between the wound datasets themselves moves with the seed as well ({{bs.P10.other.range}} for the original architectures). Leaving out any one modern method gives benchmark agreement between {{lomo.M19.bench.and}}. Comparisons between generator families also changed with the pool and the references (Online Resource 1, Table S5f).");
