H2("Rankings among modern models are weak and noisy");
P("Among the {{n.M19}} modern models, the four wound datasets agree at {{a.dice.M19.wound}} on average ({{a.dice.M19.wound.m}}; hierarchical {{a.dice.M19.wound.h}}), and the AZH control agrees with AZH at {{a.dice.M19.ctrl}} (hierarchical {{a.dice.M19.ctrl.h}}). Under NSD wound-pair agreement is {{a.nsd.M19.wound}} ({{a.nsd.M19.wound.m}}; hierarchical {{a.nsd.M19.wound.h}}). The models are close together: on each wound dataset the {{n.M19}} method means lie within a band {{sp.range}} Dice wide, and the median gap between neighbours in the ranking is {{sp.gap}}.", { after: 100 });
P("Training noise is large (Fig. 3a, Table 5). Two seeds of the same {{n.C16}} architectures, scored on the same wound images, agree at {{sd.rbar}} on average (hierarchical interval {{sd.rbar.h}}), from {{sd.rbar.range}} on the individual datasets, against {{sd.rbar.ctrl}} on the control images. Retraining one architecture with another seed moves its dataset mean with a pooled standard deviation of {{sp.seedsd}} Dice, several times the median gap between neighbours. The pre-specified rule required same-dataset cross-seed agreement above {{c.rule.hi}}. It was not met ({{sd.rbar}}). Cross-seed agreement was also not as low as cross-dataset agreement ({{sd.cross}}), so neither branch of the rule applies, and the comparison of the two that follows was not specified in advance.", { after: 100 });
P("The datasets nevertheless disagree more than the seeds do. Agreement between different wound datasets at different seeds is {{sd.cross}} (hierarchical {{sd.cross.h}}), so the gap to same-dataset agreement is {{sd.gap}} ({{sd.gap.m}}; hierarchical {{sd.gap.h}}). The gap was {{sd.gap.P9}} (hierarchical {{sd.gap.P9.h}}) for the nine original architectures and {{sd.gap.N7}} (hierarchical {{sd.gap.N7.h}}) for the seven new ones. The difference between these two gaps, {{rv.gapdiff}} ({{rv.gapdiff.m}}), is too uncertain to attribute the gap to one subgroup; the new architectures also agreed less across seeds on the same dataset ({{sd.rbar.N7}} against {{sd.rbar.P9}}). Part of the gap reflects the shared test images: on random halves of each dataset, same-dataset cross-seed agreement was {{rv.half.shared}} when both seeds were scored on the same half and {{rv.half.indep}} when they were scored on different halves. Under NSD the gap is {{sdn.gap}} ({{sdn.gap.m}}). Wound-pair agreement was {{sd.single}} for single seeds (mean over three) and {{sd.avg}} for the seed averages, a difference of {{sd.avgminus}} ({{sd.avgminus.m}}), an interval too wide to tell whether averaging seeds helps. The mean Dice of a single-seed model has a reliability (ICC) of {{sp.icc1}} on the wound datasets, and a three-seed average one of {{sp.icc3}} (point values; Online Resource 1, Table S4j).", { after: 100 });
P("The level of agreement also depends on how thresholds are set (Fig. 3b). With each model at its own FUSeg-tuned threshold, the protocol used throughout, wound-pair agreement is {{a.dice.M19.wound}}. With one common threshold of {{thr.c50.t}} for all models it rises to {{thr.c50}} ({{thr.c50.m}}; change {{thr.c50.d}}, {{thr.c50.d.m}}), and the rise has an interval above zero for every common threshold from {{thr.above.lo}} to {{thr.above.hi}}, reaching {{thr.max}} at {{thr.max.t}}. A common threshold lets the ranking follow how confident each model's outputs are: how well a model's tuned threshold predicts its rank on the wound datasets (mean Spearman correlation) is {{cal.default}} under the main protocol and {{cal.c50}} at a common threshold of {{thr.c50.t}}. With a per-dataset oracle threshold for every model, chosen on the evaluation images, agreement is {{thr.oracle}} ({{thr.oracle.m}}), not distinguishable from the main protocol (change {{thr.oracle.d}}, {{thr.oracle.d.m}}). On Medetec and CO2Wounds-V2 most oracle thresholds sit at the lowest grid value ({{rv.orc.med}} and {{rv.orc.co2}} of the models), so this setting reduces calibration differences only in part.");
