H2("Robustness of the findings");
P("The pooled-set findings survive the analysis choices (Online Resource 1, Table S6g). In all {{rp.UM19.ncond}} conditions (three post-processing pipelines under Dice and NSD, four further NSD tolerances, and FUSeg at {{d.maxside}} pixels under Dice and NSD), U+M19 wound-pair agreement is {{rp.UM19.pooled}} and exceeds the higher within-group value by {{rp.UM19.gap}}, each time with an interval above zero; agreement between wound and other datasets is {{rp.UM19.vsother}}; and stratified agreement ({{rp.UM19.strat}}) equals the size-weighted within-group average in every condition. The weaker U+F effect does not reach this standard in any condition (excess over the higher within-group value {{rp.UF.gap}}). The modality difference of every pooled set stays above zero in all conditions (U+M19 {{rp.UM19.mdiff}}).", { after: 100 });
P("Among modern models, wound-pair agreement stays within {{rob.M19.dice.range}} under Dice (post-processing pipelines, resampled FUSeg and oracle threshold) and {{rob.M19.nsd.range}} under NSD (post-processing pipelines, tolerances and resampled FUSeg). Apart from the common threshold described above, no change has an interval that excludes zero; the largest was the oracle threshold ({{thr.oracle.d}}, {{thr.oracle.d.m}}), so changes of that size cannot be ruled out. Post-processing barely reorders the modern models under Dice (lowest rank correlation between pipelines on a dataset above the floor {{rob.pp.minrho}}), more under NSD ({{rob.pp.minrho.nsd}}). Excluding empty reference masks leaves the modality differences unchanged (U+M19 {{ne.UM19.mdiff}}, M19 {{ne.M19.mdiff}}) and wound-pair agreement nearly so (U+M19 {{ne.UM19.wound}}, M19 {{ne.M19.wound}}).");

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ discussion
H1("Discussion");
H2("What a validity figure measures");
P("A rank correlation between a synthetic benchmark and clinical data is usually read as a property of the benchmark. In our data the same benchmark scores {{b.UM19.mean.bench}} or {{b.M19.mean.bench}} depending on whether classical methods are in the pool, and {{b.U.co2.bench}} or {{b.U.azh.bench}} among untrained methods depending on which wound dataset is the reference. The high pooled value comes from the gap between groups. A benchmark on which modern models beat classical ones, as they do on every wound dataset, will correlate with any of those datasets over a pooled set, whether or not it can order the modern models among themselves. The stratification identity makes this exact: once the difference in level between groups is removed, what remains is the average of the within-group agreements, and among modern models that average is low.");
P("Ordering modern models among themselves is the question a vendor, a hospital or a regulator usually faces. Among modern models the wound datasets agree with each other at only {{b.M19.mean.other}}, and the benchmark agrees with them at {{b.M19.mean.bench}}. The interval of the difference runs from {{b.M19.mean.diff.m}}, so our data cannot tell whether the benchmark is a better or a worse proxy than another clinical dataset. When clinical datasets agree this weakly, a validation study against them has little power to show that a benchmark is good, and a validity figure is informative only next to the agreement between clinical datasets for the same methods.");
H2("Modern models: dataset differences, training noise and thresholds");
P("Three things limit agreement among modern models. First, the models are close: the median gap between neighbouring models is below a hundredth of Dice on each wound dataset, much less than retraining moves a model, as reported for other benchmarks [@bouthillier2021]. Two seeds of the same architectures rank them only moderately alike on one dataset. Second, the datasets may differ. Over the {{n.C16}} architectures two wound datasets disagree more than two seeds do (gap {{sd.gap}}, hierarchical {{sd.gap.h}}). The gap was larger for the original architectures than for the new ones, but the two subgroups cannot be told apart at these sizes; part of the gap reflects image sampling, because two seeds scored on the same images share it; and for the original architectures seed and session cannot be separated. The pre-specified rule was not met, so the evidence supports only this narrower statement. Third, the threshold protocol matters. A common threshold raises agreement, but the raised ranking follows how confident each model's outputs are [@guo2017], a trait that carries across datasets, rather than which model segments wounds best; an oracle threshold, which reduces calibration differences, did not raise agreement. Our main protocol tunes each model's threshold on held-out images from its training domain, as is usual before deployment, and the low agreement applies to that protocol.");
P("For practice, a single dataset, a single training seed and a single threshold protocol can each change which modern model looks best. In our data the best model differed between wound datasets and changed with the seed, the synthetic benchmark's choice changed with the seed, a model chosen on one wound dataset did only modestly better on another than a random choice, and with single trained models hardly better. Selecting on the test set from the training clinic was worse than a random choice, which is the situation of a hospital that accepts a vendor's internal test result. Mean Dice and the share of missed wounds also pointed to different models.");
H2("Relation to prior work");
P("The composition effect is Simpson's paradox [@simpson1951;@kievit2013], and its role in benchmark agreement is established for language models [@perlitz2024] and for robustness of image classifiers, where models trained on different data lie on different lines relating in-distribution and out-of-distribution accuracy [@miller2021;@fang2022;@teney2023]. Our contribution is to measure its size in medical segmentation; to show that it inflates agreement between datasets of the modality on which the groups differ most in level, here wound photography, as well as creating disagreement across modalities; to give the exact identity behind stratification; and to show the consequence for validating synthetic benchmarks. That training seeds and small test sets make model comparisons uncertain has been argued for machine learning benchmarks and for medical imaging [@bouthillier2021;@varoquaux2022]; here the seed variation is large enough to change the validity figure of a benchmark and the model it selects. The task dependence of synthetic-to-real transfer reported for CT [@tushar2026b] and the strong transfer reported for programmable lesion trials [@tushar2026a] are consistent with our finding that validity is not a fixed property of a synthetic set. Reporting intervals over methods follows recommendations to quantify uncertainty in medical imaging evaluation [@wiesenfarth2021;@christodoulou2024]. Finally, the advice to maximise variation in synthetic data comes from training, where domain randomisation helps models transfer to real images [@tobin2017;@tremblay2018;@prakash2019]. A benchmark has a different job: it should order methods as the deployment data would, which argues for matching the deployment distribution rather than exceeding it.");
H2("Why wound photography differs");
P("Colonoscopy datasets from three centres agree strongly among the untrained and Kvasir-trained methods, while wound datasets do not. Colonoscopy frames share a device class and a field of view. Wound photographs have no standard framing: the median lesion fraction of the four wound datasets ranges from {{frac.wound.range}}, two are different crops of one clinic's photographs, and one was taken on smartphones in a leprosy programme. The AZH crops are mostly black padding, and that framing is associated with whether the pretrained models detect the wound at all. Dice depends on lesion size [@raina2023;@reinke2024]. Under NSD, wound-pair agreement rises from {{a.dice.U.wound}} to {{a.nsd.U.wound}} among untrained methods and from {{a.dice.F.wound}} to {{a.nsd.F.wound}} among from-scratch methods, but falls from {{a.dice.K.wound}} to {{a.nsd.K.wound}} among Kvasir-trained methods, so framing conventions may contribute to the disagreement [@torralba2011;@kendrick2022], but the evidence is mixed.");
H2("Recommendations");
list([
  "Report a benchmark's agreement within the group of methods it is meant to compare, and name that group.",
  "Report agreement against several clinical datasets, as a matrix, together with the agreement between those clinical datasets and a within-dataset control.",
  "Report intervals that resample methods and the images or photographs of each dataset.",
  "For trained models, train several seeds and report how well the ranking on one dataset is reproduced across seeds before interpreting differences between datasets.",
  "State the threshold protocol, because a common threshold can create agreement that reflects calibration rather than segmentation quality.",
  "Report failure rates, such as the share of missed lesions, next to mean Dice.",
  "Do not select a model on a test set from the training clinic alone; check the choice on at least one external dataset.",
  "If a mixed pool cannot be avoided, report the within-group agreements directly; stratified agreement is their size-weighted average and adds nothing beyond them.",
]);
H2("Limitations");
list([
  "The modern sets are small ({{n.M19}} methods; {{n.C16}}, {{n.P9}} and {{n.N7}} architectures in the seed analyses), so Spearman correlations move in coarse steps and intervals are wide. Several statements about modern models rest on intervals that include zero.",
  "The pretrained models and nnU-Net differ in input resizing, augmentation, training length and threshold, so their comparison is between two pipelines, not between architectures.",
  "In the second session {{n.manet.fail}} of the {{n.manet.run2}} MA-Net trainings diverged under mixed precision and were not imputed. MA-Net on ResNet-18 is averaged over two seeds and MA-Net on ResNet-50 is a single model, so these columns carry more training noise than the others, although they are the best seed-averaged models on Medetec and FUSeg.",
  "Seed {{d.seed0}} of the ten original architectures was trained in a different GPU session from seeds {{d.seeds12}}, so for these architectures the effects of the session and of the seed cannot be separated. The first session did not record the PyTorch and timm versions.",
  "nnU-Net was trained once, in its 2D configuration, for {{d.nn.ep}} epochs rather than the default {{c.nn.ep.default}}, with its threshold fixed at {{d.nn.thr}}. Its scores carry the noise of a single training, which matters because it was the synthetic benchmark's pick in most settings. The pretrained architectures were trained on {{n.train}} of the {{sift.fus.train}} FUSeg training images at {{d.lb}} pixels; they represent current architectures, not every deployed system.",
  "The level of agreement among modern models depends on the threshold protocol. Our statements about low agreement apply to each model at its own tuned threshold; a common threshold gives higher agreement, and the oracle threshold, chosen on the evaluation images and limited by the grid, does not remove calibration differences.",
  "The modern models score near zero Dice on all seven non-wound datasets, so their colonoscopy agreement and modality difference rank floor-level scores, and the from-scratch group is at the floor on most of these datasets too. The untrained methods are at the floor on FUSeg (group mean {{md.U.fus}}), so their wound-pair agreement includes rankings of near-zero scores on that dataset; without FUSeg it is {{rv.U.nofus}}.",
  "The synthetic benchmarks are parametric, contain {{d.syn.n}} images each and were built at three synthetic seeds; the benchmark analyses rest on one generator family at one texture amplitude. Learned generators such as diffusion models were not tested. Benchmark intervals for sets that include untrained or from-scratch methods resample methods only.",
  "A fifth wound dataset was excluded before scoring because many of its masks did not match their images. Wound photography is therefore represented by four datasets from three sources, two of which share one clinic, and statements about wound datasets rest on {{n.pairs.wdiff}} pairs from different sources. Of the kept AZH test images, {{ov.azh.unused}} are crops of FUSeg photographs that no model saw, so AZH and FUSeg are related beyond sharing a clinic.",
  "Medetec's {{n.img.med}} images are {{n.unit.med}} photographs with four variants each, so its effective sample is smaller than its image count; the AZH sets also contain several crops per photograph.",
  "Images were not harmonised to one resolution across datasets, and NSD uses a fixed pixel tolerance, which is looser at lower resolution. Resampling FUSeg and varying the tolerance did not change the conclusions, but other datasets were not resampled. Untrained methods and probability-output methods receive different post-processing, which coincides with the main contrast between groups.",
  "The seed, benchmark, selection and robustness analyses are exploratory and are not corrected for multiple comparisons. Intervals for selection losses resample images with the candidates fixed, and both losses are measured against the best observed model, which is biased upwards. The link between padding, scale and misses is correlational.",
  "SAM and MedSAM received box prompts from the reference masks, an oracle that makes their scores incomparable with automatic segmentation; they were therefore kept out of every method set.",
], false);

H1("Conclusions");
P("Agreement between clinical datasets about which segmentation method is better depends strongly on which methods are compared. Pooling classical and modern methods inflates agreement between wound datasets and turns agreement between wound and other datasets negative, and a synthetic benchmark validated over such a pool inherits the inflation. Among the modern models that practitioners choose between, wound datasets agree weakly, training noise is of the same order as the differences between models, and the threshold protocol shifts the level of agreement. When clinical datasets agree weakly with each other, a validation against them can say little about how well a synthetic benchmark orders the models. Validity should be reported within the group of methods to be compared, against several clinical datasets, with intervals over methods and images, and with the training seeds and threshold protocol stated.");

